# Episode 01 - Your First Plugin

Hello! and welcome to the introduction to audio software development!
In this episode, you're going to build your first complete plugin:
a hard-clipper!

{Title Card}

## Course Materials

Before we get started, let's download the materials we need for the
course.

If you click the link in the description of this video, it will take
you to the companion GitHub repository. Don't worry if you're not
familiar with GitHub. I'll show you exactly what you need to do to
get all the goods.

Click on the green Code button, and download the ZIP. This will
download all of the course materials as a ZIP folder. Open your
downloads folder, and extract the zip. You can do this on Mac by
just double clicking it. Move this folder to a safe location.
We're going to be using it a lot!

## Downloading JUCE

Back in the companion repo, if you scroll down to the Episodes
section, you'll see a brief description of each episode. And
if you click on the name of one of them, you'll see a page
with links to resources used in the episode, or places that
you can go to develop your knowledge further if a topic is of
interest to you.

In the Episode 1 page, click on the JUCE website link to be taken
to the JUCE homepage. Clock on the "Get JUCE" button to be taken
to the download page. Click the download button under the free plan.
Then, simply click on your operating system to download JUCE!
Like with the course materials, drag the JUCE folder into your
development location.

## Creating a JUCE Project

Now it's finally time to start creating our first plugin.

Open the JUCE folder and double click the Projucer. If it comes up
with a warning, press Open Anyway. 

We're building a basic plugin, so let's give it a name. I'll call it
asdHardClip. There should be no spaces in the project name! Under
exporters, make sure XCode is selected if you are on Mac, or 
Visual Studio is selected if you are on Windows. If you're on Linux,
you figure it out you probably know what you're doing. There are other
methods of building JUCE plugins, but if you're just getting started,
these are by far the easiest. Leave the modules how they are, and
let's create the project!

Navigate to your development folder and create a new folder for all
the plugins you're going to be making. Open it, and save your project
there.

Let's configure our plugin a bit before we write some code. Click the
pink cog wheel to bring up the plugin settings. There are a few important
ones, so let's go through them.

1. Project Version: for now, set it to 0.0.0. As you keep working on your
plugin and make new releases, increment this version number.
2. Company Name & Copyright: Obviously this is for copyright. Put your name
and the year here.
3. Plugin Formats: xxxxx
4. Plugin Characteristics: xxxxx
5. Name: This is the name of the plugin that will be displayed in the DAW.
6. Description: A brief description of what your plugin does.
7. Manufacturer & Manufacturer Code: Some DAWs categorize plugins by
manufacturer. These fields tell the DAW what category to put them in.
8. Plugin Code: A unique identifier for your plugin. No two plugins
from the same manufacturer can have the same plugin code.
9. AUType & VST3/AAX Category

Now that we've configured our project, go to the course materials
folder and open the Templates and Materials folder. Open the
JUCE_Startup file for the future, and copy the two .h files.
Navigate to your project folder and paste the .h files into source.

Back in the Projucer, if you click on the file explorer, those
files we just added aren't showing up yet! We can add them by
selecting the source folder, clicking the pink plus icon, and
choosing "Add existing files." Select the files we just added and
press open.

Perfect! Now that all our files are in place, we can open XCode
by clicking on its logo here.

## Using the Template

Now, the default code that JUCE gives you is pretty awful
to start with in my opinion, so I've made some starter code to
get us started much quicker.

I have the template open on the right and our project on the left.
There are directions in the comments of the template, but let's 
walk through them real quick to make sure we get it right.

1. **Snippet 1:** In PluginProcessor.h, copy the two `#include`
lines to the top of the file with the other `#include`s. Then,
take the rest *excluding* the public text, and paste it at the
bottom of the existing public section.

2. **Snippet 2:** In PluginProcessor.cpp, at the top where the
constructor is, add a comma after this closing parenthesis, and
copy this apvts line after the last `#endif`.

3. **Snippet 3:** Take the entire thing and paste it below
the destructor. Then change where it says `YourPlugin` to have
the name of your plugin, matching the prefix of all the other
functions.

4. **Snippet 4:** Take just the inner contents of the
`prepareToPlay` function and paste them into the `prepareToPlay`
in your project.

5. **Snippet 5:** Do the same as snippet 4, but with the
`processBlock` function. Replace the contents only with the
template.

6. **Snippet 6:** Replace the contents only of the
`getStateInformation` and `setStateInformation` functions.
In the `createEditor` function, comment out the existing line
and add the line in the template under it.

I know that was a lot, but that's the template. Unfortunately,
you'll have to do that at the start of every plugin you make.
However, you'll get faster at it over time, and it's still
far, FAR faster than setting all that up manually.

Finally, we can get to writing our little hard clipper.